%[text] # lspsys Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Loudspeaker system, including its environment.
%[text] **Namespace:** none (global namespace)
%[text] **Superclasses:** none
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] The `lspsys` class models a loudspeaker system: a driver (`comp.Driver`) mounted in an enclosure (`comp.Enclosure`) that radiates into its environment, which is described by the speed of sound, the density of air and a reference pressure. All quantities are calculated in the frequency domain, for the frequencies in `Frequency`.
%[text] The system is solved as a chain of two-port networks: electrical, transduction, mechanical, diaphragm, and the acoustic front and rear side of the diaphragm (see `solve2PortNetwork`). `createResult` returns the outcome as a `result` object.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] obj = lspsys
%[text] ```
%[text] Creates a loudspeaker system with default properties: 1000 logarithmically spaced frequencies from 20 Hz to 20 kHz, a source voltage of 2.83 V, and a `comp.ClosedBox` enclosure. The driver in that enclosure has all its parameters set to zero, so set them before calling `createResult`; otherwise the results are `NaN`.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] The properties are listed in the order of the class definition file.
%%
%[text] ### `Frequency` — Frequency vector
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[Hz\]
%[text] Frequencies at which the system is calculated. All values must be positive. Default: 1000 logarithmically spaced values from 20 Hz to 20 kHz, `logspace(log10(20),log10(20e3),1000)`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `SourceVoltage` — Voltage of the source
%[text] `(1,1) double` \[V\]
%[text] Voltage of the source that drives the voice coil of the driver. Must be positive. Default: 2.83 V, which delivers 1 W into a resistive load of 8 Ω.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `RadiationAngle` — Radiation angle
%[text] `(1,1) string`
%[text] Radiation angle of the diaphragm into the environment. Currently only `"2pi"` is supported: radiation into a half space, as for a rigid piston in an infinite baffle. Default: `"2pi"`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `Enclosure` — Enclosure with driver
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Enclosure`
%[text] Enclosure that the driver is mounted in. The driver itself is the property `Enclosure.Driver`. Default: `comp.ClosedBox`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `AngularFrequency` — Angular frequency
%[text] `double` \[rad/s\]
%[text] Angular frequency $ \\omega = 2\\pi f $ for every element of `Frequency`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `WaveNumber` — Wave number
%[text] `double` \[rad/m\]
%[text] Wave number, the spatial angular frequency: $ k = \\frac{\\omega}{c} = \\frac{2\\pi f}{c} = \\frac{2\\pi}{\\lambda} $, with the speed of sound $ c $ from `SpeedOfSound`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `Wavelength` — Wavelength
%[text] `double` \[m\]
%[text] Wavelength $ \\lambda = \\frac{c}{f} $ for every element of `Frequency`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `NumFrequencies` — Number of frequencies
%[text] `double`
%[text] Number of elements in `Frequency`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `SpeedOfSound` — Speed of sound
%[text] `double` \[m/s\]
%[text] Speed of sound in air: 343 m/s.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Constant`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `AirDensity` — Density of air
%[text] `double` \[kg/m3\]
%[text] Density of air at sea level: 1.225 kg/m3.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Constant`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ### `ReferencePressure` — Reference pressure
%[text] `double` \[Pa\]
%[text] Reference pressure for sound pressure level calculations: 20e-6 Pa.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Constant`, `Hidden`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | [`solve2PortNetwork`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))) | Instance | Solve the two-port network of the system. Returns a struct with the diaphragm volume velocity and the source current. |
%[text] | [`createResult`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','createresultdoc','createresultdoc.m'))) | Instance | Solve the system and return a `result` object. |
%[text] | `f2k` | Static | Wave number for a frequency: `2*pi*f / SpeedOfSound`. |
%[text] | `f2Lambda` | Static | Wavelength for a frequency: `SpeedOfSound ./ f`. |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Plot the electrical impedance
%[text] Create a driver according to the specifications of a B&C 21SW152-8, mount it in a closed box of 200 liter, and plot the electrical impedance of the loudspeaker system.
d = comp.Driver;
d.Re = 6;
d.Le = 2.2e-3;
d.Qes = 0.38;
d.Qms = 6.4;
d.Fs = 32;
d.Sd = 1680e-4;
d.Vas = 200e-3;
e = comp.ClosedBox;
e.RearVolume = 200e-3;
e.Driver = d;
sys = lspsys;
sys.Enclosure = e;
res = sys.createResult;
semilogx(res.Frequency,abs(res.ElectricalImpedance),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:73997a87]
grid on %[output:73997a87]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:73997a87]
ylabel("Electrical impedance [\Omega]") %[output:73997a87]
title("Electrical impedance of the loudspeaker system") %[output:73997a87]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Use a different frequency range
%[text] Continue with the system of the previous example. Calculate 400 frequencies between 10 Hz and 1 kHz to look at the resonance around 30 Hz.
sys.Frequency = logspace(log10(10),log10(1e3),400);
res = sys.createResult;
semilogx(res.Frequency,abs(res.ElectricalImpedance),LineWidth=1.5) %[output:10382052]
grid on %[output:10382052]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:10382052]
ylabel("Electrical impedance [\Omega]") %[output:10382052]
title("Electrical impedance around the resonance") %[output:10382052]
%%
%[text] ### Example: Wave number and wavelength
%[text] The static methods calculate the wave number and the wavelength for any frequency, here 1 kHz.
waveNumber = lspsys.f2k(1000) %[output:1a14e705]
wavelength = lspsys.f2Lambda(1000) %[output:76db38d1]

